"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][2]={"school_physics_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – Light sources, propagation and shadows<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Why does the shadow of a hand grow when the hand moves towards a\ntorch? First distinguish a <strong>light source<\/strong> (such as a\nlamp) from an <strong>illuminated object<\/strong> that sends back\nincident light. We see an object when light from it reaches our eyes. In\nthe simple setting, light propagates approximately in straight lines\nthrough a uniform transparent medium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Straight-line propagation and shadows<\/h2>\n<p>In the <strong>ray model<\/strong>, lines with arrows indicate the\ndirection of propagation. A light ray is not a thin glowing thread; it\nis a geometric aid. An opaque object blocks light paths behind it. An\nideal <strong>point source<\/strong> produces a sharply bounded\n<strong>shadow region<\/strong>; its cross-section on a screen is the\n<strong>shadow image<\/strong>. An extended source also produces a\n<strong>penumbra<\/strong> because an obstacle blocks some, but not all,\nparts of the source.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Two boundary rays define\nthe shadow region from a point source to a screen. The drawing is\nschematic.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A point source sends two boundary rays past an opaque obstacle; a sharply bounded shadow reaches a screen.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A point source sends two boundary rays\npast an opaque obstacle; a sharply bounded shadow reaches a\nscreen.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Point source shadow: similar triangles enlarge the\nprojection.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A point source, a centred object of height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a parallel screen share an axis. The object is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=20\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the source, and the screen is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom it. Similar triangles give the height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the shadow:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>H<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{H}{h}=\\frac{b}{a},\\qquad H=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\cdot\\frac{60\\,\\mathrm{cm}}{20\\,\\mathrm{cm}}=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas no units. If the screen moves to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile the other positions stay fixed, the shadow becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntall. These results apply to the simplified point-source model, not\nautomatically to every real lamp.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Shine an ordinary LED torch on a piece of cardboard in front of a\nwall. Keep the lamp fixed, move the wall, and describe changes in shadow\nsize and edge sharpness. Do not use lasers or focused beams, and never\nshine powerful lights towards the eyes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish a light source from an illuminated object.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=3\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=25\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=100\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a large lamp often make the shadow edge fuzzy?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a drawn ray represent physically?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A source emits light; an illuminated object reflects or scatters\nincident light.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\cdot100/25=12\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different parts of the source illuminate different regions,\nproducing a penumbra.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It represents a propagation direction, not a visible material\nline.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of the model and next step<\/h2>\n<p>The simple model ignores diffraction and, in the point-source case,\nthe physical size of lamps. Next we investigate <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reflection<\/a> from a smooth\nsurface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/optik\">Sichere\nSchule – Optics: Safety in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light sources, lasers\nand optical radiation; precautions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Reflection at plane mirrors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A smooth mirror sends incident light in a well-defined reflected\ndirection. A matte wall usually scatters light across many directions.\nBoth surfaces can reflect light, but the geometry of a <strong>plane\nmirror<\/strong> is particularly easy to predict.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The law of reflection and the normal<\/h2>\n<p>At the point of incidence, draw the <strong>normal<\/strong>, a line\nperpendicular to the reflecting surface. Both the angle of incidence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the angle of reflection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare measured <strong>from the normal<\/strong>, not from the mirror\nsurface. For an ideal plane mirror:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><menclose notation=\"box\"><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/menclose><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boxed{\\alpha=\\beta}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane. This\ndirectional change can be demonstrated with a mirror and weak,\nspread-out illumination; no laser is needed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> An incoming and outgoing\nray are symmetric about the dashed normal; arcs indicate the angles.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_008_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Incident and reflected rays with dashed normal; angles alpha and beta are equal and measured to the normal.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Incident and reflected rays with dashed\nnormal; angles alpha and beta are equal and measured to the\nnormal.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At a plane mirror the angles of incidence and reflection,\nmeasured to the normal, are equal.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Virtual image and worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An object\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin front of a plane mirror appears the same distance\n<strong>behind<\/strong> it. This is a <strong>virtual image<\/strong>:\nlight from the front of the mirror does not actually pass through and\nconverge behind it. The eye traces the reflected rays backwards. The\nimage is upright and the same size; the reflection reverses the\ndirection perpendicular to the plane of the mirror.<\/p>\n<p>A ray incident at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>35<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">35^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>to the normal<\/strong> is reflected at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>35<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">35^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the normal. If the incident angle is instead given as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>55<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">55^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>to the mirror surface<\/strong>, the corresponding angle to the\nnormal is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>55<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>35<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ-55^\\circ=35^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation idea<\/h2>\n<p>Place a hand mirror on a table, mark a perpendicular normal on paper,\nand compare input and output directions using two paper markers. Use\nindirect low-intensity illumination; laser investigations require the\nteacher’s separate safety assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If the incidence angle is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhat is the reflected angle?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A ray arrives at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>70<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the surface. Find the incidence angle to the normal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a virtual image not be captured on a screen behind the\nmirror?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give one difference between a plane mirror and white paper.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the normal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No real rays converge there through the mirror; they only appear to\noriginate behind it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A plane mirror reflects directionally, whereas paper scatters light\ndiffusely.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Surface roughness affects the distribution of reflected light. When\nlight crosses into another transparent material, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">refraction<\/a> usually occurs\nalongside reflection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/optik\">Sichere\nSchule – Optics: Safety in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light sources, lasers\nand optical radiation; precautions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – Refraction and total internal reflection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A drinking straw seems bent at the water surface. The straw is\nunchanged; the light changes direction at the boundary between water and\nair. This directional change is called <strong>refraction<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ray model at a boundary<\/h2>\n<p>Light travels at different speeds in different transparent materials.\nWe describe the effect using the <strong>refractive index<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen light enters glass or water obliquely from air, it normally bends\n<strong>towards the normal<\/strong>. Going from glass into air, it bends\n<strong>away from the normal<\/strong>. At normal incidence there is no\ndirectional change. Some light can also be reflected.<\/p>\n<p>For an exact calculation in suitable conditions, <strong>Snell’s\nlaw<\/strong> states:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1\\sin\\alpha=n_2\\sin\\beta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Both angles are measured from the normal. This equation is an\n<strong>extension<\/strong>; lower-secondary learning begins with\nqualitative explanation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A horizontal air–glass\nboundary shows the incoming and refracted rays and the normal. The\ndrawing is not to scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_009_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A blue incident ray in air reaches glass and is refracted in orange towards the normal.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A blue incident ray in air reaches glass\nand is refracted in orange towards the normal.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>At an air-to-glass boundary an oblique ray refracts towards the\nnormal.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Total internal reflection and optional calculation<\/h2>\n<p>When light travels from a medium with a higher refractive index to\none with a lower index, then above a <strong>critical angle<\/strong> no\nrefracted ray can emerge: <strong>total internal reflection<\/strong>\noccurs. This cannot happen for a ray travelling from air into glass.<\/p>\n<p>Using approximate values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{air}}\\approx1.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{glass}}\\approx1.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor light travelling <strong>from glass to air<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1.00<\/mn><mn>1.50<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>41.8<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\alpha_{\\mathrm{c}}=\\frac{1.00}{1.50}\\approx0.667,\\qquad \\alpha_{\\mathrm{c}}\\approx41.8^\\circ.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At the critical angle the limiting refracted direction lies along the\ninterface. Above it, reflection can guide light along optical fibres.\nThe numerical result depends on the assumed indices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Put a drinking straw in a glass of water, view it from the side at\ndifferent positions, and identify what looks different and what remains\nthe same. Use ordinary room light, not focused sunlight or lasers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does an oblique ray bend when entering glass from air?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is there no direction change at normal incidence?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can total internal reflection occur for a ray travelling from air\ninto water?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Extension: for glass-to-air, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_{\\mathrm{glass}}=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>50<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=50^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis total internal reflection expected?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Towards the normal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The direction already follows the normal; both angles are zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No, not when entering the material with the higher index.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>50<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msup><mn>41.8<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50^\\circ&gt;41.8^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Refractive index can depend on wavelength, leading to colour\nseparation. Ray paths are an approximation. Curved transparent surfaces\nlead us to <a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">lenses<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Lenses and focal points<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A magnifying glass makes small print appear larger. Under different\nconditions, a lens can form an inverted image on a screen. The type of\nimage depends on the lens and the object’s position.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Converging and diverging lenses<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>converging lens<\/strong> (convex, usually thicker in the\nmiddle) brings rays parallel to the optical axis to an ideal\n<strong>focus<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the far side. A <strong>diverging lens<\/strong> (concave, usually\nthinner in the middle) spreads parallel rays apart; tracing these rays\nbackwards gives a virtual focal point on the incident side. The distance\nfrom the lens plane to the focal point is the <strong>focal\nlength<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere we assign positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a converging lens and negative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a diverging lens.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>thin-lens approximation<\/strong> ignores lens thickness\nand assumes small ray angles to the axis. A parallel ray passes through\nthe image-side focus of a converging lens. A ray through the centre is\ntreated as undeviated. Two construction rays can locate an image\npoint.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Parallel rays converge\nafter a converging lens and diverge after a diverging lens; dashed\nbackwards extensions reveal the virtual focus.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_010_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parallel rays meet after a converging lens on the left and spread after a diverging lens on the right.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parallel rays meet after a converging\nlens on the left and spread after a diverging lens on the\nright.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Converging lenses focus parallel rays; diverging lenses spread\nthem apart.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optional worked calculation<\/h2>\n<p>For a thin converging lens in air, object distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand focal length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=10\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nour sign convention gives:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>f<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1f=\\frac1g+\\frac1b\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\frac1b=\\frac1{10\\,\\mathrm{cm}}-\\frac1{30\\,\\mathrm{cm}}=\\frac1{15\\,\\mathrm{cm}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The real inverted image forms at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=15\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbehind the lens. Signed lateral magnification is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B/G=-b/g=-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nhalf-size and inverted. This is an <strong>optional\ncalculation<\/strong>, beyond the basic qualitative ray sketch.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Use a school converging lens to view an ordinary, non-dazzling\nilluminated object. A teacher may capture its image on a screen and move\nthe screen to find focus. <strong>Never focus sunlight using a magnifier\nor lens:<\/strong> this creates eye and fire hazards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which lens converges rays parallel to the axis?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the focus of a diverging lens virtual?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish focal length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand image distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Extension: find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=12\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=24\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A converging lens.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Outgoing rays do not meet; only their backwards extensions do.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncharacterises the lens;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso depends on object distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/b=1/12-1/24=1/24\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=24\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Real lenses have aberrations such as colour fringing or edge blur.\nNext we compare image planes in the <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">eye and a camera<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/optik\">Sichere\nSchule – Optics: Safety in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light sources, lasers\nand optical radiation; precautions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Image formation in the eye and camera<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A photograph can be sharp or out of focus. The eye must also adjust\nits optical properties for objects at different distances. Both systems\nneed light, imaging optics and a light-sensitive surface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Forming and focusing an image<\/h2>\n<p>A converging lens can form a <strong>real image<\/strong> on a\nreceiving surface. In the eye, the cornea and lens refract light, while\nthe <strong>retina<\/strong> detects it. The eye lens changes its\ncurvature and optical power by <strong>accommodation<\/strong>. The\neyeball’s length stays approximately constant during this process.<\/p>\n<p>In a camera, an objective lens – usually made of multiple elements –\nfocuses an image on a <strong>sensor<\/strong>. Focus may be adjusted by\nmoving lens elements. The <strong>pupil<\/strong> limits light entering\nthe eye, while the <strong>aperture<\/strong> has a comparable role in a\ncamera. The actual optical image is inverted in both cases, although we\ndo not perceive our world upside down and camera images can be oriented\nelectronically.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> Rays cross the axis after\na converging optical system and reach a retina or sensor in an inverted\narrangement.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_011_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Left, a schematic eye with cornea, lens and retina; right, camera body with objective and sensor and ray paths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Left, a schematic eye with cornea, lens\nand retina; right, camera body with objective and sensor and ray\npaths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The eye and camera form an image on a light-sensitive\nsurface.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison and example<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature<\/th>\n<th>Eye<\/th>\n<th>Digital camera<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Light-sensitive surface<\/td>\n<td>Retina<\/td>\n<td>Image sensor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focusing<\/td>\n<td>Change in eye-lens curvature<\/td>\n<td>Adjustment of the lens system or its position<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light control<\/td>\n<td>Pupil and iris<\/td>\n<td>Aperture, exposure time and electronic processing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Thought experiment:<\/strong> Move an object towards a camera\nwithout changing its lens settings. The image plane required for sharp\nfocus shifts. If the sensor position is unchanged, the image is blurred\nuntil the camera refocuses. The analogy explains function; a single-lens\nsketch is not a complete eye model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation<\/h2>\n<p>Photograph a near and a distant object in ordinary room light.\nRefocus on each and describe what appears sharp. Never look towards the\nSun through camera lenses, binoculars or magnifiers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name the light-sensitive surfaces in the eye and in a camera.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does a typical eye focus on near objects?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does “real image” mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the pupil not the same thing as the retina?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Retina and image sensor.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By changing the curvature and optical power of the eye lens.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Light rays actually reach the image plane and could form an image on\na screen.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The pupil is the entrance opening; the retina receives light and\nconverts stimulation into neural signals.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limits and next step<\/h2>\n<p>Our simplified model omits the cornea, multi-element camera optics,\nimage processing and visual defects. Further possibilities and limits\nare treated in <a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Optical\ninstruments<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/optik\">Sichere\nSchule – Optics: Safety in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light sources, lasers\nand optical radiation; precautions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M2-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M2-L6 – Optical instruments and their limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Light, shadows and geometrical optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A magnifier makes print look bigger. Yet a blurry printout does not\nreveal unlimited new detail if it is enlarged further. Optical\ninstruments require two separate concepts:\n<strong>magnification<\/strong> and <strong>resolution<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three instruments and two different properties<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>magnifying glass<\/strong> is a converging lens that can\nproduce an upright, virtual enlarged image at a suitable object\ndistance. A <strong>telescope<\/strong> uses an objective and an eyepiece\nto make small angular sizes of distant objects easier to observe. A\n<strong>light microscope<\/strong> uses multiple lenses to examine tiny\nnearby structures; its objective usually creates a magnified\nintermediate image for the eyepiece.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Magnification<\/strong> describes the apparent size of an\nobject or image. <strong>Resolution<\/strong> describes whether two\nclosely spaced details can still be seen as separate. It depends, among\nother things, on light wavelength, effective aperture and optical\ndefects. Increasing magnification alone does not restore information\nthat was never resolved.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original teaching diagram:<\/strong> A pattern becomes larger,\nwhile two nearby details are distinguished in one example but not\nreliably separated in another. Dots are schematic, not measured optical\npoint-spread functions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_012_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A pattern with larger dots on the left; on the right two distinct and two overlapping details.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A pattern with larger dots on the left;\non the right two distinct and two overlapping details.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Magnification and resolution are different properties.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application and interpretation<\/h2>\n<p>A book illustration is enlarged fourfold on a screen, but the\noriginal picture has very few pixels. Existing pixels become larger or\nnew pixels are inferred by software; the original capture does not\nsuddenly hold four times as many <strong>observed<\/strong> details. In\nphysical optics, diffraction and lens aberrations limit usable\ndetail.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instrument<\/th>\n<th>Typical use<\/th>\n<th>Important limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Magnifying glass<\/td>\n<td>Near small print<\/td>\n<td>Focus, field of view and eye distance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Telescope<\/td>\n<td>Distant objects<\/td>\n<td>Aperture, atmospheric turbulence and light gathering<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light microscope<\/td>\n<td>Small nearby structures<\/td>\n<td>Wavelength, numerical aperture and specimen quality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation<\/h2>\n<p>Look at a fine newspaper print through an ordinary magnifier, then\ncompare a blurry patch at the same magnification. <strong>Never look\ndirectly at the Sun or view it through optical devices; never focus\nsunlight using lenses.<\/strong> Laser demonstrations at school require a\nteacher-led risk assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the difference between magnification and resolution?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why choose a telescope rather than a magnifying glass for a distant\ntarget?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might a highly magnified image remain blurry?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two factors limiting fine detail in a real optical device.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Magnification describes image size; resolution concerns the\nseparation of nearby details.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A telescope increases the angular size of distant targets.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Enlargement also enlarges existing blur; missing detail does not\nreappear automatically.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Examples include diffraction or wavelength, aperture, lens\naberrations and atmospheric turbulence.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Geometrical ray optics describes many images, but resolution limits\nrequire the wave model of light. We treat this qualitatively here.\nFurther optics topics such as dispersion and colour perception belong to\nlater paths or optional extensions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1 – Sound sources and\nvibrations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.schulentwicklung.nrw.de/lehrplaene/lehrplan/208/g9_ph_klp_%203411_2019_06_23.pdf\">Ministry\nof Education NRW – Lower secondary physics curriculum (2019)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nContent areas 4/5, reflection, refraction, lenses, eye and instruments ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.sichere-schule.de/physik/praktisches-arbeiten/optik\">Sichere\nSchule – Optics: Safety in physics lessons<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Light sources, lasers\nand optical radiation; precautions · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
